1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
A toolset gives you a few decisions that quietly make or break routing.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,000
How you name tools, whether any overlap, and how many you expose all shape whether the

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
model picks correctly.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This lesson walks through naming, overlap, and the token cost of tools, and also when to prune. Let's dive in.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Names are the first thing the model scans, so make each one unambiguous.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Give every tool a distinct, action-oriented name that says what it does.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
When your tools span several services, namespace them by prefixing the service, so related

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
tools group together and don't collide.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
A name like BillingListInvoices tells the model far more than a bare list, which could

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
mean almost anything.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Consistent service resource patterns across your set cut confusion, especially once you're

12
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
using tool search to surface tools on demand.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The goal is to shrink the gap between what the model wants to do and what it actually picks.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Now, the description is still the primary routing signal, just as you learned earlier.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:14,000
But in a multi-server setup, a distinct, namespaced name removes ambiguity before the description is even read.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Overlap is the quiet killer of tool routing.

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Picture two distinct tools, each owning one clear job, with descriptions that don't compete.

18
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
The model maps a request straight to the obvious choice.

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Now picture two tools whose descriptions blur into each other.

20
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
The model has no clean way to tell them apart, so it misselects, and that gets worse when

21
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
the names look similar too.

22
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
This is why adding a tool is never free.

23
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:47,000
A new tool that overlaps an existing one doesn't just add capability, it adds ambiguity to

24
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
every decision nearby.

25
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Before you add one, check that it doesn't step on a tool you already have.

26
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
If it does, consolidate, instead of stacking.

27
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
When the model calls the wrong tool, it usually traces back to three sources.

28
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
First, similar names.

29
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
When two tools read almost the same, the model confuses them and picks the wrong one under pressure.

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Second, competing descriptions.

31
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
When two descriptions overlap, there's no clean signal for which tool fits the request,

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
so the choice becomes a coin flip.

33
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Third, vague scope.

34
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
A tool that tries to do everything matches too many requests, so it fires when a sharper,

35
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
more specific tool should have handled the job.

36
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Notice the fix for all three is the same instinct.

37
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Sharpen the names, separate the descriptions, and tighten each tool's scope until every

38
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
choice is obvious.

39
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Tools aren't free, and it's worth knowing the price.

40
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
The moment you provide tools, the API injects a tool use system prompt into every request.

41
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,000
On Opus 4.8, that's roughly 290 tokens of overhead, and the tool names, descriptions,

42
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
and schemas all count as input tokens on top of that.

43
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Here's the part people miss.

44
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Even setting tool choice to none still pays this cost once tools are present.

45
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
The genuinely zero token case is providing no tools at all, not turning them off with a parameter.

46
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
None of this means avoid tools.

47
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
It means a big tool set spins context before the conversation even starts.

48
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
So every tool you keep should earn that ongoing cost.

49
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Pruning is as important as adding, and three signals tell you when to cut.

50
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
First, rarely correct.

51
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
If your evals show a tool is seldom the right call, its token cost and its ambiguity outweigh

52
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,000
whatever it adds.

53
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Second, redundant.

54
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
If another tool already covers the action, fold them together and drop the duplicate

55
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
rather than keeping both.

56
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Third, unused surface.

57
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Every extra tool widens the attack surface.

58
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
So remove anything the task doesn't actually need.

59
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
The instinct to resist is hoarding tools because more feels like more capability.

60
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:03,000
It usually isn't.

61
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
A leaner set the model routes cleanly through beats a bloated one that leaves it guessing on every turn.

62
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Sometimes an agent genuinely needs dozens of tools, and loading them all up front burns

63
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
context and blurs selection.

64
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,000
There's a mechanism for this.

65
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:27,000
The tool search tool lets Claude discover tools on demand instead of holding every definition in context.

66
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
You mark rarely used tools with defer loading set to true.

67
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:39,000
So they load only when the model reaches for them, while you keep the few most used tools always loaded.

68
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
The effect is that a large surface stops crowding the context window and the model

69
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:47,000
reasons over a smaller active set at any moment.

70
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:52,000
I won't tie this to specific benchmark numbers, since those shift.

71
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The mechanism is the point.

72
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Discovery on demand is how large tool sets stay manageable.

73
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Tool search is the first scaling lever, and it's already working for you.

74
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Two more levers build on it once a tool set gets large.

75
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
First, programmatic calling.

76
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
It lets Claude orchestrate tools through code, which keeps intermediate results out of the

77
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
context window instead of piling them into the conversation.

78
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Note it's first party API only, not available on Bedrock or Vertex.

79
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Second, tool use examples.

80
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Attaching a few example inputs to a tool sharpens accuracy when its parameters are complex and

81
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
hard to describe in prose alone.

82
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
You won't need these on a small set, but as the surface grows, they're how you keep

83
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,000
routing accurate without paying for every tool on every turn.

84
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:48,000
One last decision.

85
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Not every tool belongs locked inside a single app.

86
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Some capabilities are worth sharing across many clients, and that's the call MCP is for.

87
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Keep app-specific tools as plain custom tools in the one app that uses them.

88
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
But when several clients need the same set, package it behind an MCP server.

89
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,000
That gives you one standardized model-agnostic integration instead of re-implementing the

90
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
same tools in every app.

91
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
The deciding question isn't how many tools you have.

92
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
It's whether the capability has to travel beyond a single client.

93
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
If it does, MCP is the clean way to expose it.

94
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,000
If it doesn't, a local custom tool is simpler and lighter.

95
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Right-sizing a tool surface comes down to naming, overlap, and count.

96
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:42,000
It matters because distinct names and non-overlapping tools route correctly, while every tool you

97
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
add spends more tokens.

98
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
One thing to watch.

99
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:53,000
You should prune rarely-used tools, defer load, and reserve a shared server for capabilities

100
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
that clients reuse over and over.

101
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
That wraps up naming, overlap, and tool count tradeoffs.

102
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
When you're ready, let's jump into the next video.

